**Does My Business Really**\
**Need a Website?**

Your\
business is on social media, customers can reach you on WhatsApp, and you're\
already making sales. So, do you really need a website?

It's a\
fair question.

Perhaps\
your business already has an Instagram page, a Facebook account, or a WhatsApp\
Business profile. Maybe most of your customers come through referrals, and\
business has been going well without a website.

If that's\
the case, investing money in a website might seem unnecessary, especially when\
you have other business expenses to worry about.

But\
here's something worth considering.

**What happens when a potential customer wants to learn more about your**\
**business, but you don't have a central place where they can find everything**\
**they need to know?**

That's\
where a website can make a real difference.

Let's\
look at what a website can actually do for your business, when you need one,\
and whether it's worth the investment.

**1. Your Social Media Page Isn't the Same as a**\
**Website**

Social\
media has become an important tool for businesses. It helps you showcase your\
products, connect with customers, share updates, and attract new audiences.

There's\
absolutely nothing wrong with using it.

However,\
your social media presence and your website serve different purposes.

Think of\
social media as a place where people discover and interact with your business.\
Your website, on the other hand, can serve as a central destination where they\
learn about your business, explore your services, view your work, and take the\
next step.

Imagine\
someone recommends your bakery to a potential customer.

The\
customer searches for your business online but finds only a social media page\
with scattered posts, old promotions, and limited information about your\
products.

They\
might have to send you a message to ask about your menu, prices, delivery\
options, and location.

Now\
imagine that same customer visiting your website and immediately finding your\
menu, product images, opening hours, location, and ordering information.

Which\
experience makes it easier for that customer to make a decision?

A\
well-designed website can help you provide that second experience.

And when\
you make it easier for people to understand your business, you remove\
unnecessary obstacles between interest and a potential sale.

You can\
continue using social media to attract attention while directing interested\
customers to your website for more information.

The two\
can work together.

**2. A Website Helps Build Trust and Credibility**

Before\
spending money on a product or service, people often want to know whether they\
can trust the business they're dealing with.

This is\
particularly important when customers are discovering your business for the\
first time.

They may\
have questions such as:

- Is\
  this business legitimate?
- Does\
  it have experience in what it offers?
- Can\
  I see examples of its work?
- How\
  do I contact the business if something goes wrong?
- Why\
  should I choose this business over another?

A\
professional website gives you a place to answer these questions.

You can\
introduce your business, explain your services, display customer testimonials,\
showcase previous projects, and provide clear contact information.

For a\
fashion brand, this might mean displaying product collections and size guides.

For a\
consultant, it could mean presenting professional experience, services, and\
client results.

For a\
school, it might include information about its facilities, curriculum,\
admissions process, and contact details.

The goal\
isn't simply to have a beautiful website.

It's to\
give potential customers enough relevant information to understand your\
business and feel more confident about taking the next step.

Of\
course, a website alone doesn't guarantee trust. Your business still needs to\
deliver quality products, provide good customer service, and keep its\
information accurate.

But a\
well-built website can help you communicate your professionalism more\
effectively.

**3. Your Business Can Be Found Beyond Social Media**

Imagine\
someone searching Google for a service you offer in their area.

A business\
owner might search for a web designer in Lagos, while a parent might search for\
a private school in their neighbourhood.

If your\
business has a relevant, well-optimised website, it has an opportunity to\
appear in those search results.

Without a\
website, you may miss some of those opportunities.

This is\
where search engine optimisation, commonly called SEO, becomes useful.

SEO\
involves improving your website and its content to help search engines\
understand your pages and connect them with relevant searches.

For\
example, a bakery in Lagos could publish useful information about its cakes,\
custom orders, delivery areas, and celebration packages.

A digital\
agency could publish articles answering common questions about website development,\
branding, and online visibility.

When\
these pages provide genuinely useful information and are properly optimised,\
they can help businesses reach people actively looking for what they offer.

However,\
it's important to understand that **having a website doesn't automatically put**\
**your business on the first page of Google.**

Search\
visibility takes time. Relevant content, technical optimisation, competition,\
and other factors all play a role.

The\
advantage is that your website gives you a foundation for building that\
visibility.

You can\
also explore our article on [Generative Engine\
Optimisation (GEO) and optimising your website for AI search](https://emmystack01.com/articles/generative-engine-optimisation-geo-how-to-optimise-your-website-for-ai-search) to learn\
more about improving how your business information can be discovered online.

Instead\
of depending entirely on people stumbling across your social media posts, you\
can work towards attracting people who are already searching for your products\
or services.

**4. A Website Can Help You Save Time**

How often\
do customers ask you the same questions?

What's\
the price?

Where are\
you located?

What\
services do you offer?

How can I\
place an order?

Do you\
deliver?

These\
questions are normal, but answering every one individually can consume a\
significant amount of your time.

A website\
can help reduce that workload.

You could\
create dedicated pages for your services, pricing information, frequently asked\
questions, delivery policies, booking processes, and other details customers\
regularly request.

Instead\
of explaining everything from scratch, you can direct customers to the relevant\
page.

For\
example, if someone asks about your services on WhatsApp, you could share a\
link to your services page.

If a\
customer wants to understand how your booking process works, you can direct\
them to a page that explains the steps.

Depending\
on how your website is built, you could also include online booking, order\
forms, payment integrations, or other features that reduce manual work.

This\
doesn't mean a website will eliminate the need to communicate with customers.

Rather,\
it helps you handle routine enquiries more efficiently, giving you more time to\
focus on running your business.

**5. You Can Turn Your Website Into a Sales Tool**

A website\
doesn't have to be a digital brochure that people visit and leave.

When\
designed around your business goals, it can help move potential customers from\
discovering your business to making a purchase or enquiry.

Consider\
an example.

You run a\
business offering professional photography services.

Someone\
discovers your work through Instagram and wants to know more.

They\
visit your website and find your portfolio, photography packages, customer\
testimonials, and booking information.

After\
reviewing everything, they complete an enquiry form or book a consultation.

Your\
website has helped guide that person through several steps without requiring\
you to explain everything manually.

Depending\
on your business, your website could help you:

- Generate\
  enquiries from potential customers.
- Accept\
  bookings and appointment requests.
- Sell\
  products directly online.
- Collect\
  leads for follow-up.
- Display\
  your portfolio and previous projects.
- Promote\
  specific services or offers.
- Direct\
  visitors to WhatsApp for conversations and orders.

The right\
features depend on how your business operates.

A\
restaurant may benefit from an online menu and reservation system. An\
e-commerce business may need a product catalogue, shopping cart, and secure\
payment integration. A service provider may need a portfolio and enquiry form.

**The goal is not to build a website with as many features as possible.**\
**It's to build one that helps your business achieve specific goals.**

If you're\
exploring website options for your business, visit [Emmy STACK01's website solutions page](https://emmystack01.com/websites) to see\
where to start.

**6. Your Business Needs a Digital Home You Can**\
**Control**

When you\
build your business entirely around a social media platform, your online\
presence depends heavily on that platform.

Changes\
to its algorithm, policies, features, or account access can affect how you\
reach your audience.

Your\
website gives you another channel through which customers can discover and\
interact with your business.

You can\
organise your information, create pages around your services, publish articles,\
showcase your work, and direct visitors towards the actions you want them to\
take.

You also\
have greater control over your website's design, content, and functionality,\
depending on your hosting arrangement and the tools you use.

However,\
owning a website doesn't mean you own every part of its infrastructure. Your\
domain registrar, hosting provider, and other third-party services still have\
their own terms and limitations.

That's\
why it's important to use reliable providers and maintain access to your\
domain, hosting, website files, and relevant accounts.

Your\
website works best as part of a broader digital strategy.

You can\
continue using social media to attract attention, WhatsApp to communicate with\
customers, and your website to provide detailed information and support\
enquiries or sales.

These\
channels don't have to compete with one another. They can work together.

**7. But Does Every Business Need a Website Right**\
**Now?**

Here's\
the honest answer: **not every business needs a website immediately.**

If you're\
just starting out, have a very limited budget, and are still testing whether\
people want your product, you might be better off focusing on getting your\
first customers.

A\
WhatsApp Business profile, social media page, and simple way to receive\
payments or orders may be enough to get started.

You don't\
necessarily need to invest in a complex website before you've validated your\
business idea.

However,\
as your business grows, your needs may change.

A website\
becomes increasingly worth considering when:

- Customers\
  regularly ask for more information about your business.
- You\
  want to reach people beyond your existing social media audience.
- Your\
  competitors have websites that help them attract customers.
- You're\
  spending too much time answering repetitive questions.
- You\
  need a professional place to showcase your products or services.
- You\
  want to publish content that can attract search traffic over time.
- You're\
  ready to accept bookings, generate leads, or sell products online.
- You\
  want to build a stronger and more consistent online presence.

The\
important thing is to assess your actual business needs rather than building a\
website simply because other businesses have one.

And\
remember, you don't have to start big.

A simple,\
well-designed website with the right information can be more useful than an\
expensive website filled with features your business doesn't need.

**8. How Much Should You Invest in a Business**\
**Website?**

The cost\
of a website depends on several factors, including its design, functionality,\
content requirements, integrations, and ongoing maintenance.

A basic\
business website with a homepage, services page, about page, and contact\
information will generally require less investment than a custom e-commerce\
platform with inventory management, customer accounts, and payment\
integrations.

Before\
deciding on a budget, ask yourself three questions.

**First, what do I want my website to achieve?**

Are you\
trying to build credibility, attract enquiries, showcase your work, accept\
bookings, or sell products online?

Your\
answer will help determine which features you actually need.

**Second, what does my business need today?**

Avoid\
paying for complex features simply because they sound impressive.

Start\
with what will make the biggest difference to your customers and your business\
operations.

**Third, what will it cost to maintain the website?**

Remember\
to consider domain renewal, hosting, security, updates, and any paid tools or\
integrations your website requires.

A website\
is an investment, and like any business investment, its value depends on how\
well it supports your goals.

Rather\
than asking only how much a website costs, consider what it could help your\
business accomplish and whether that potential value justifies the expense.

